\item \textbf{Cell B} (truncated observation $+$ episode-level advantage): Window size $h{=}2$ with a single episode-level advantage shared across all windows. Only Mechanism 1 is active; sign misattribution is eliminated because all windows in an episode receive the same advantage sign.
\item \textbf{Cell D} (full observation $+$ episode-level advantage): The complete trajectory is processed as a single sequence with episode-level advantage. Neither mechanism is active.
\end{itemize}

Comparing Cell A against Cell B isolates the effect of sign misattribution while holding information loss constant. If Cell B exhibits more stable training dynamics than Cell A, the difference is attributable to Mechanism 2. Comparing Cell B against Cell D isolates the effect of information loss while both conditions use episode-level advantage. The performance gap between these two cells reflects how truncated context limits the policy's generalization to tasks with cross-step dependencies.

This factorial design tests qualitative predictions about training dynamics, not quantitative effect decomposition. We expect Cell A to exhibit the most unstable training (oscillation or collapse), Cell B to train more stably but plateau below Cell D due to the information constraint, and Cell D to achieve monotonic improvement. Section~\ref{sec:experiments} reports the results.

\subsection{Selective Verification}
\label{sec:selective_verification}

We propose selective verification as a direction for future investigation. At each step, the agent would output a binary decision $v_t \in \{\texttt{VERIFY}, \texttt{SKIP}\}$ before generating its action, and the training signal would use an action-change indicator (whether verification actually altered the action) to reward informative verification. This component has not been implemented and is proposed as a direction for future investigation. Episode-level training serves as a prerequisite: training a selective verification policy requires a base agent with sufficient task competence, which step-independent training cannot produce.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Generality of Sign Misattribution}
\label{sec:discussion_generality}

The sign misattribution mechanism identified in Section~\ref{sec:m2_sign_misattribution} is not specific to RAGEN, ALFWorld, or GRPO. The failure arises from three structural properties that co-occur in any multi-turn agent RL setup: (a) step-level credit assignment via windowing, (b) sparse binary episode reward, and (c) group-relative advantage normalization. When these three conditions hold, the advantage sign for each window collapses to a proxy for episode outcome regardless of local action quality (Equation~\ref{eq:sign_correlation}). The environment details, the specific RL algorithm, and the model architecture are irrelevant to this structural argument.

Consider the broader landscape of LLM agent training. Web agents operating in WebArena~\cite{zhou2024webarena} execute 20 to 40 interaction steps per episode with binary task completion rewards. Scientific experimentation agents in ScienceWorld~\cite{wang2022scienceworld} follow multi-step experimental protocols where success is determined only at the final measurement. GUI agents in mobile and desktop environments issue sequences of clicks and keystrokes with success defined by whether the target UI state is reached. Tool-use agents chain API calls where only the final output is evaluated. All of these settings combine long trajectories with sparse terminal rewards, and any framework that decomposes these trajectories into independently optimized windows will exhibit sign misattribution.

Proposition~2 (Section~\ref{sec:formal_probability}) reveals a further implication: the severity of misattribution scales with task difficulty. As the baseline success rate $p$ decreases, $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \to p$ in the large-$K$ limit. Environments with longer horizons or more complex reasoning requirements tend to have lower baseline success rates, so sign misattribution becomes \textit{more} severe precisely where credit assignment is most needed. A web agent with $p = 5\%$ baseline success would experience correct gradient direction in only 5\% of updates, a regime where training cannot converge regardless of other hyperparameter choices.

\subsection{Direction Correctness vs.\ Credit Granularity}
\label{sec:discussion_tradeoff}

Episode-level GRPO eliminates sign misattribution by treating the full trajectory as a single optimization sample. The cost of this choice is that all tokens in the episode receive the same advantage. A correct action at step 3 and a redundant action at step 40 are reinforced with identical magnitude and sign. This represents a trade-off between two desiderata: direction correctness (whether the gradient sign is aligned with local action quality) and credit granularity (whether the gradient magnitude reflects per-step contribution to success).

At the low success rates typical of early training ($p = 12.5\%$ in our experiments), direction correctness dominates. When 80.9\% of gradient updates point in the wrong direction, the optimization actively destroys good behaviors. Uniform reinforcement of all steps in a successful episode is imprecise but at least preserves correct sub-strategies within the trajectory. The asymmetry is stark: coarse-but-correct gradients produce slow convergence, while fine-but-wrong gradients produce divergence.

As the policy improves and $p$ increases, the marginal value of per-step credit assignment grows. At $p = 50\%$, Proposition~2 predicts $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \approx 50\%$, meaning step-independent training would produce a roughly unbiased gradient in expectation (though with high variance). In this regime, the coarseness of episode-level training may become a binding constraint on sample efficiency. Methods that combine episode-level direction correctness with finer-grained credit represent a natural extension. GAE with episode-level correction (using a value function for magnitude estimation while constraining signs to be consistent with episode outcome) and GiGPO's nested group structure~\cite{feng2025gigpo} are two concrete approaches. The connection to GAE(1,1) established in Section~\ref{sec:classical_connection} suggests a principled interpolation: begin with $\lambda = 1$ (episode-level) during early training when direction correctness matters most, and anneal toward $\lambda < 1$ as the policy stabilizes and per-step credit becomes valuable.

\subsection{Relationship to Classical Credit Assignment}
\label{sec:discussion_classical}

As established in Section~\ref{sec:classical_connection}, step-independent windowing falls below the TD(0)-to-MC credit assignment spectrum, creating disconnected optimization targets with no temporal coupling. This structural failure has a revealing analogue in cooperative multi-agent RL, where shared team reward creates the same credit assignment challenge. Solutions such as counterfactual baselines (COMA~\cite{foerster2018coma}) and value decomposition (QMIX~\cite{rashid2018qmix}) address this by estimating individual contributions to the team outcome. In multi-turn LLM agent training, the same structural problem arises from an implementation choice rather than from the problem formulation itself. The multi-agent credit assignment problem is inherent to team games; the sign misattribution problem in agent RL is artificial, introduced by the windowing design and removable by episode-level training.

\subsection{Dual Mechanism Interaction}
\label{sec:discussion_interaction}

Information loss (Mechanism 1) and sign misattribution (Mechanism 2) are not independent. They interact through the success rate $p$, creating a feedback loop that accelerates collapse at small window sizes. Mechanism 1 restricts the representable strategy space, imposing a ceiling on achievable task success for a given $h$. This ceiling determines the maximum $p$ that the policy can sustain. Mechanism 2 then drives performance below this ceiling at a rate that depends on $p$ itself: lower $p$ produces more severe misattribution (Proposition~2), which further reduces $p$, which further increases misattribution severity.

This interaction creates a vicious cycle. At $h = 2$, the information-loss ceiling is low, so the policy cannot achieve high $p$ even with perfect optimization. The low $p$ triggers severe sign misattribution ($\approx$80.9\% wrong-direction gradients at $p = 12.5\%$), which erodes whatever competence the model retains within its truncated context. The erosion reduces $p$ further, intensifying the misattribution. The two-phase collapse observed empirically (strategic failure followed by formatting failure) reflects this cascade: Mechanism 2 first destroys task-level strategies (reducing $p$ to near zero), then continues to corrupt lower-level action representations that were initially robust.

At larger $h$ values, the ceiling is higher, sustaining a higher $p$ that partially mitigates misattribution severity. This predicts a more gradual degradation rather than catastrophic collapse. The h-sweep ablation (Section~\ref{sec:results_hsweep}) tests this prediction by examining whether larger $h$ values exhibit slower collapse trajectories. Episode-level training breaks the cycle entirely by eliminating both mechanisms simultaneously: the full context removes the information-loss ceiling, and the single episode-level advantage removes the opportunity for sign misattribution.


\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Single environment.} Our experiments are conducted exclusively on ALFWorld, a text-based household environment. The dual mechanism analysis depends only on the training algorithm structure (windowing, binary reward, group normalization) and not on environment-specific properties, so the theoretical argument is environment-agnostic. However, empirical validation on additional multi-turn environments such as ScienceWorld~\cite{wang2022scienceworld} or WebShop~\cite{yao2022webshop} would strengthen the generality claim. Different environments may exhibit different baseline success rates, trajectory lengths, and dependency structures that modulate the practical severity of sign misattribution.

\paragraph{Single model scale.} All experiments use Qwen3-1.7B. The severity of sign misattribution depends on $p$ (Proposition~2), which varies with model capacity. A 7B or 14B model may achieve a higher SFT baseline, reducing the fraction of wrong-sign updates. The misattribution mechanism does not disappear at higher $p$ (it persists as long as $p < 1$), but its practical impact may be less catastrophic if the model starts closer to the ceiling. Scaling experiments are needed to characterize the relationship between model capacity, baseline success rate, and collapse dynamics.

\paragraph{Coarse credit in episode-level training.} Episode-level GRPO assigns uniform advantage to all tokens in an episode. For very long episodes ($T > 100$) or tasks with many independent sub-goals, this coarseness may limit learning efficiency even though it preserves direction correctness. The trade-off between direction correctness and credit granularity (Section~\ref{sec:discussion_tradeoff}) becomes increasingly relevant at higher success rates where the sign error is less severe but the efficiency cost of uniform reinforcement grows.

\paragraph{Selective verification not implemented.} The selective verification component (Section~\ref{sec:selective_verification}) remains a design proposal. We present the training methodology diagnosis and episode-level solution, but the downstream application of training a selective verification policy has not been implemented or evaluated. The contribution of this paper is the credit assignment analysis and fix, not the verification application.

\paragraph{Limited gradient analysis scope.} The empirical sign misattribution verification (Section~\ref{sec:sign_verification}) uses $h = 2$, representing the most extreme case of windowed training. Analysis across multiple $h$ values ($h \in \{5, 10, 20\}$) would reveal how misattribution severity varies with window size in practice and whether the theoretical predictions (which are $h$-independent for a given $p$) hold when different window sizes produce different effective $p$ values during training.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{RL Training for LLM Agents.}
The ReAct paradigm~\cite{yao2023react} established interleaved reasoning and acting as the standard framework for LLM agents. Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} emerged from mathematical reasoning alignment and was scaled to large systems through techniques such as dynamic sampling and clip-higher stabilization~\cite{dapo2025}. Adapting GRPO to multi-turn interactive agents requires addressing the mismatch between its episode-level design and the practical constraints of long trajectories. RAGEN~\cite{ragen2025} introduced the step-independent windowing approach that has become the community default, partitioning trajectories into fixed-size windows optimized separately. Subsequent work has proposed algorithmic improvements that operate within or alongside this framework. GiGPO~\cite{feng2025gigpo} addresses credit assignment through a nested group structure that estimates step-level advantages without a learned critic, achieving substantial gains on ALFWorld and WebShop. Self-Guide~\cite{selfguide2026} co-evolves an internal reward model with the policy, providing denser learning signals than binary episode outcomes. SPEAR~\cite{spear2025} combines self-imitation learning with progressive curriculum to handle sparse rewards in early training. DigiRL~\cite{digiRL2024} demonstrates autonomous online RL for device-control agents, achieving substantial gains over supervised fine-tuning. WebAgent-R1~\cite{wei2025webagent-r1} extends GRPO to multi-turn web interaction (M-GRPO) with dynamic context compression, while SORL~\cite{li2025sorl} introduces turn-level importance sampling for off-policy stability. HCAPO~\cite{hcapo2026} uses an LLM-as-critic to assign hindsight credit at critical decision states. These methods improve training algorithms or reward design at various granularities. None diagnoses the step-independent windowing mechanism itself as a source of systematic sign misattribution, which is the structural failure we identify and formalize.

\paragraph{Credit Assignment in Reinforcement Learning.}
The temporal credit assignment problem has a long history. REINFORCE~\cite{williams1992reinforce} provides unbiased but high-variance episode-level gradients. PPO~\cite{schulman2017ppo} reduces variance through a learned value function and clipped surrogate objectives. Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE)~\cite{schulman2016gae} interpolates between one-step bootstrap and Monte Carlo returns via the $\lambda$ parameter, offering a continuous spectrum from TD(0) to full-episode credit. The TD($\lambda$) family~\cite{sutton1988td} established this interpolation in the tabular setting. In the LLM agent context, process reward models provide step-level supervision: AgentPRM~\cite{choudhury2025agentprm,xi2025agentprm} estimates per-step value through Monte Carlo rollouts or TD-based estimation with GAE. SkillMaster~\cite{skillmaster2026} proposes DualAdv-GRPO, which separately estimates advantages for task-solving and skill-editing decisions. In cooperative multi-agent RL, the analogous problem of shared team reward has been addressed through counterfactual baselines (COMA~\cite{foerster2018coma}) and value decomposition (QMIX~\cite{rashid2018qmix}), which estimate individual agent contributions to the team outcome. Episode-level GRPO, as we advocate, is equivalent to GAE(1,1) without a critic: it assigns uniform advantage across all steps, forgoing per-step granularity in exchange for correct gradient direction. Our contribution is not a new credit assignment algorithm but a diagnosis of why the community's standard adaptation of GRPO (step-independent windowing) produces catastrophic sign errors in multi-turn settings.

\paragraph{Selective Verification and Adaptive Computation.}
A growing body of work trains models to allocate computation adaptively rather than uniformly. In the reasoning domain, Thinkless~\cite{du2025thinkless} introduces an RL-trained control token that decides whether to invoke extended thinking for each query. AutoThink~\cite{tu2025autothink} uses multi-stage RL to shape when reasoning-capable models activate their chain-of-thought. SelfBudgeter~\cite{selfbudgeter2025} frames adaptive computation as token budget allocation trained via budget-guided GRPO. In the agent domain, ARES~\cite{yang2026ares} trains an SFT-initialized router that selects reasoning effort levels per step, refined via GRPO with a combined outcome and token-efficiency reward. Agent-Omit~\cite{agent-omit2026} learns to omit redundant thoughts and observations through agentic RL, reducing context length without sacrificing performance. Sherlock~\cite{sherlock2025} performs selective verification at the workflow level through offline counterfactual analysis and speculative execution. Derailer-Rerailer~\cite{wan2025derailer-rerailer} detects instability triggers in reasoning chains and invokes targeted verification. These approaches operate at the reasoning-token level (Thinkless, AutoThink, SelfBudgeter), workflow-DAG level (Sherlock), or use heuristic triggers (Derailer-Rerailer) and SFT-initialized routers (ARES). Our selective verification differs in scope and mechanism: it makes a binary verify-or-skip decision at each agent interaction step, trained end-to-end via RL with an action-change informativeness signal that requires no external annotations or heuristic design.

\paragraph{Self-Verification and Self-Correction.}
Self-verification has received significant attention as a mechanism for improving LLM reliability. Kamoi et al.~\cite{kamoi2024self-correction-survey} survey self-correction in LLMs and establish that pure intrinsic self-correction (without external feedback) is unreliable across most settings. Long et al.~\cite{long2026selfverification} quantify the verification dilemma empirically: over 90\% of self-verification instances are confirmatory, with only approximately 2\% producing corrective changes. CorrectBench~\cite{tie2025correctbench} further demonstrates that uniform self-correction exhibits diminishing returns, as models repeatedly ``correct'' already-correct answers. VerifiAgent~\cite{verifiagent2025} proposes a unified verification framework with meta-verification and tool-based adaptive strategies, focusing on improving verification quality through better methods. These findings collectively establish two facts: verification quality is limited by confirmatory bias, and uniform application of verification wastes computation on uninformative instances. Our work addresses a complementary question: given that most verification is confirmatory, \textit{when} should an agent verify? The 90\% confirmatory rate reported by Long et al. directly motivates learning a selective policy that triggers verification only at steps where action change is likely.

